In this lesson we'll look at solving one-variable inequalities, which are a lot like solving equations, but with a twist. An inequality compares two expressions using a less-than, greater-than, less-than-or-equal-to, or greater-than-or-equal-to sign. And instead of getting a single answer, you'll usually find a whole set of values that work. The big idea is that you solve it using the same steps you'd use for an equation, but you have to remember one critical rule. If you multiply or divide both sides by a negative number, the inequality sign flips around. For example, if you have three times x less than twelve, dividing by positive three gives x less than four, no flip needed. But if you have negative three times x less than twelve, dividing by negative three gives x greater than negative four, because the sign flips. Adding or subtracting any number keeps the direction the same. Multiplying or dividing by a positive number also keeps the direction. Only a negative multiplier changes things. The main slip to avoid is dividing by a negative and forgetting to flip the sign. Now let's see it in action.